00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:47.530
So a brief history of
containers and Kubernetes.

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:49.960
So containers have been
around for a long time.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:54.540
They've been a part of
Linux for quite a while.

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:57.620
We really started to see
container adoption pick up

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:00.320
with the introduction
of Docker in about 2013.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:04.910
And that was a key moment where
public adoption of containers

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:06.410
really started to take off.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:10.190
Docker offered a lightweight
container runtime

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:13.077
and an amazing packaging
and deployment model

00:08:13.077 --> 00:08:15.410
where you could bundle your
app and all its dependencies

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:18.720
and deploy that consistently
across different environments.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.630
Now, when Docker came
out, they were really

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:23.840
focused on that dev workflow
and solving the packaging

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:27.800
and deployment for a single
node across a single container

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:28.850
as well.

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:32.929
And we all realized that it
wasn't the full solution.

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:36.679
The eventual goal would be
to manage multiple containers

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:38.299
across many different nodes.

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:41.419
And that's when Kubernetes
really started to--

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:44.530
the seeds of Kubernetes started
to get planted at Google

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:47.330
was around 2013 time frame.

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:51.140
So Kubernetes really at its
heart is an orchestration tool.

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:52.430
Now, what does that mean?

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:54.800
It really means that you
can package and deploy

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.780
multiple containers
across multiple hosts,

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:58.400
and Kubernetes
takes care of most

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:00.890
of the heavy lifting for you
with things like scheduling,

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:04.090
and application health checking,
and more of those types of--

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:06.510
more of those types of things.

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:10.380
But in my opinion, Kubernetes
is really two powerful concepts.

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:12.380
The first is that
its an abstraction

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:14.250
over infrastructure.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:17.570
And this is really, really
important for your developers.

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:20.420
We wanted to make it so that
most developers didn't really

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:23.330
have to retrain their
skills depending

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:25.130
on whatever cloud
they were using

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:27.680
or if they wanted to move
from something on-prem

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:29.930
over to a cloud
platform as well.

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:32.330
Kubernetes gives you that
consistent base layer

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:34.760
that allows the operators to
worry about infrastructure

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:38.450
and the developers to get a
consistent tooling platform.

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:41.660
And the second real
powerful tool of Kubernetes

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:44.210
is that it's a declarative API.

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:46.490
Everything in
Kubernetes is driven off

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:49.070
this model of declare
your state, declare

00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:51.350
what you want to have
happen, and then Kubernetes

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:55.190
has built-in feedback loops that
will make that state reality.

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:56.760
And they're
constantly observing.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:58.850
We can write even
custom controllers

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.280
to handle a bunch of different
state reconciliations

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:05.174
for even new objects
that you want to create.

00:10:05.174 --> 00:10:06.590
And the best part
about Kubernetes

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:11.300
is that it tries to encapsulate
a bunch of best practices

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:15.140
around application
deployment and scheduling,

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:18.770
so things like, where does
a particular container need

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:20.510
to get scheduled on which node?

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:22.070
Is that node healthy?

00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:26.760
Can I do-- can I restart
that node if it goes down?

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:29.180
How do I provision storage?

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:31.670
We have storage
providers for a number

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:33.980
of different platforms
built into Kubernetes

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:36.470
through the ecosystem.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:38.589
Logging and monitoring
is also integrated.

00:10:38.589 --> 00:10:40.130
And we've seen a
lot of extensibility

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:41.960
with tools like
Prometheus and Grafana

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:44.480
that work on top of
the Kubernetes system,

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:46.550
and even going so far
as things like identity

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:47.990
and authorization.

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:50.210
There's a ton of work that
happens in the community

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:54.230
every month on adding new and
new features to the platform.

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:57.872
It's actually quite astounding
how fast Kubernetes iterates.

00:10:57.872 --> 00:10:59.330
And it's really
important to always

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:02.960
make sure you're keeping up
with the latest developments.

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:06.475
But, with great power
comes great responsibility.

00:11:06.475 --> 00:11:09.180
Kubernetes can be
a bit overwhelming.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:11.480
There's a lot of power
under the hood here.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:16.160
And there is inherent complexity
with a system as powerful

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:17.810
as Kubernetes.

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:20.600
And when we talk to
users, we typically

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:22.580
break people up into two camps.

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:25.400
One is people that are operating
the cluster, cluster operators.

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:27.650
And the second one is
application developers.

00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:29.930
And from a cluster
operator perspective,

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:33.120
installing Kubernetes is
relatively straightforward.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:36.500
The real pain comes when you
have to manage the day two

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:37.670
operations.

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:39.965
This is things like
setting up security

00:11:39.965 --> 00:11:43.700
in TLS between my nodes,
encrypting etcd and making sure

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:46.310
that no one can access it,
doing disaster recovery,

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:49.040
like backup and restore, and
even things like node lifecycle

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:51.260
management if a node goes down.

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:54.770
These are all things that
cluster operators don't always

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:56.280
like to handle themselves.

00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:58.640
And we see a lot of
questions in forum posts

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:01.800
on how to operate
Kubernetes at scale.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:05.990
One of the key missions of
GKE when it first came out

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:08.570
was to take the heavy lifting
off of the cluster operators

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:10.310
and give it to Google.

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:13.911
And that's really the big power
of Google Kubernetes Engine

00:12:13.911 --> 00:12:16.160
is that we wanted to offer
this fully managed solution

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:17.840
where Google runs
your clusters and you

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:22.450
as the developers get to
focus on the applications.

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:27.430
So GKE is our hosted,
cloud-managed Kubernetes

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:28.060
solution.

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:30.910
We were GA in August
of 2015, so we've

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:33.760
been running a number of
production clusters for three

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:34.990
years now.

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:37.180
One of the nice
things about GKE is

00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:39.140
that we take option
Kubernetes and we

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:41.590
are we try to keep as close
to upstream as possible.

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:43.900
And they're always releasing
new versions of GKE

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:46.570
as new versions of
Kubernetes are coming out.

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:48.720
But we wanted to add
deep integration with GCP

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:51.347
so we make it really easy to
use other features like logging,

00:12:51.347 --> 00:12:52.930
and monitoring, and
identity right out

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:56.950
of the box with
Kubernetes and GKE.

00:12:56.950 --> 00:12:59.410
So, let's look at the
architecture of how

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:03.080
GKE is deployed on top of GCP.

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:05.920
So, there's typically
two parts to Kubernetes,

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:07.390
two major parts to Kubernetes.

00:13:07.390 --> 00:13:09.190
We have the control
plane and then

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:11.740
we have the nodes that
make up your cluster.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:15.760
And on GKE, the control plane
is a fully managed control plane

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:17.210
controlled by Google.

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:18.730
We run this in our own project.

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:21.850
And we have a team of
site reliability engineers

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:24.040
that are constantly doing
things that I described

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:25.382
on the day two operations.

00:13:25.382 --> 00:13:26.590
They're scaling the clusters.

00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:27.370
They're health checking it.

00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:28.286
They're backing it up.

00:13:28.286 --> 00:13:31.270
They're making sure etcd is
always upgraded with the latest

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:33.059
and greatest security patches.

00:13:33.059 --> 00:13:34.600
This is really the
operational burden

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:36.670
taken off of the
end user and put

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:38.980
into the hands of
the Google engineers.

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:42.134
There are two ways to spin
up the control plane for GKE.

00:13:42.134 --> 00:13:44.050
We have the classic way,
which is zonal, where

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:45.340
we spend up a single node.

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:47.499
And just recently we
announced general availability

00:13:47.499 --> 00:13:49.540
of regional clusters,
which allows you to spin up

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:51.430
a multi-master
high-availability control

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:55.860
plane, which I'll cover in a
couple of the later slides.

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:57.570
Now, the second
piece are the nodes.

00:13:57.570 --> 00:13:59.140
And these run in your project.

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:00.150
These are where
you're actually going

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:01.380
to deploy your containers.

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:05.320
And we call these the workers
of the Kubernetes cluster.

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:08.760
Now, on GKE, we have a
concept of node pools, which

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:12.120
is a group of
homogeneous machines

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:13.380
with the same configuration.

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:15.690
I'll talk about that
on the next slide.

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:18.750
For these nodes, we are
the only cloud platform

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:21.930
to offer a fully
managed node experience.

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:25.350
And what that means is that
we provide the base image,

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:27.690
we constantly keep
it updated, and we

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:29.970
have features like node
auto repair and node auto

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:33.540
upgrade that allow your cluster
to keep revving with the latest

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:35.910
and greatest upgrades without
having you to do anything

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:38.330
at all.

00:14:38.330 --> 00:14:41.980
So, node pools are
also a GK concept.

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:45.310
And this is-- there's a lot
of advantages to building out

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:46.450
things with node pools.

00:14:46.450 --> 00:14:49.210
So, node pulls are really just
a group of related machines.

00:14:49.210 --> 00:14:50.410
It's the same configuration.

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:52.176
If I can have an
n1-standard-1 instance,

00:14:52.176 --> 00:14:53.800
I can create a node
pool of size three,

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:57.880
and that will give me three
n1-standard-1 instances.

00:14:57.880 --> 00:14:59.481
Now, what's neat
about node pools

00:14:59.481 --> 00:15:01.480
is that it makes it really
easy to mix and match

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:02.770
different instance types.

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:05.230
So you can spin up node
pool A with the standard

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:07.360
configuration, then you
can add a second node pool

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:10.090
with things like
preemptable VMs or GPUs

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:14.170
so that you can target specific
workloads to those node pools.

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:16.220
Even better, node
pools make it really,

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:20.120
really easy to add availability
to your application.

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:24.060
So if I'm running in a single
zone, let's say US-Central1-B,

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:27.890
and I want to add a
failover to US-Central1-A,

00:15:27.890 --> 00:15:31.180
all you have to do is create a
new node pool in that new zone

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:33.380
and then attach it
back to the cluster.

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:35.560
And this is just
a really nice way

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:38.260
to group related instances
together and then

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:40.880
scale out your application.

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:43.390
So let's actually walk
through what happens

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:46.670
when you upgrade a node.

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:49.049
One of the features that
we offer to GK customers

00:15:49.049 --> 00:15:50.840
is this feature called
Maintenance Windows.

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:53.180
Now, Maintenance Windows give--

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:56.390
it's basically you telling us
a four-hour window when we are

00:15:56.390 --> 00:15:58.530
allowed to upgrade your nodes.

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:01.444
So, if you have high
traffic during the day,

00:16:01.444 --> 00:16:03.860
and you want to make sure that
upgrades happen at midnight

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:05.460
or whatever, you can
specify this window.

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:07.460
We will only upgrade
your master or your nodes

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:09.510
during that time frame.

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:13.010
So, what happens is we
all go and drain a node.

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:15.732
And what this does is it
removes traffic from that node.

00:16:15.732 --> 00:16:17.440
And then we do an
operation in Kubernetes

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:20.450
is called cordon which makes
it so that new pods don't

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:21.920
get scheduled to that node.

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:23.630
At this point, we
spin up a new VM

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:25.490
on the latest version
of Kubernetes,

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:27.500
and then we attach it
back to your cluster.

00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:30.360
So we do this one by one and
a rolling upgrade fashion.

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:32.060
There is a couple
other ways to upgrade

00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:33.320
that I'm going to cover
and a little bit later.

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:35.630
But that's generally
how most of our users

00:16:35.630 --> 00:16:38.480
keep their nodes
healthy and up to date.

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:40.670
The other feature that we
offer for node management

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:42.230
is node auto-repair.

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:45.440
And node auto-repair is
basically a health checking

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:45.972
service.

00:16:45.972 --> 00:16:47.930
There's a lot of reasons
why nodes can go down,

00:16:47.930 --> 00:16:50.300
whether it's a
kernel panics or disk

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:52.690
errors, out-of-memory
errors, et cetera.

00:16:52.690 --> 00:16:54.620
Node auto-repair is
constantly monitoring

00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:58.340
the health of your cluster
for particular errors.

00:16:58.340 --> 00:17:00.640
And if the health check
fails, like it does here,

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:02.360
and it goes into
a not ready state,

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:03.860
it will go and do
the same operation

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:06.000
that we did during upgrade
where we'll drain the node,

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:08.333
we'll spin up a new one, and
then we'll reattach it back

00:17:08.333 --> 00:17:09.544
to the cluster.

00:17:09.544 --> 00:17:11.960
This makes it really easy to
make sure that your nodes are

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:13.790
always healthy and
at the set amount

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:15.460
that you want in your cluster.

00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:19.880
And as I said, all of this
is really the end journey

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:21.710
to get you to
focus on the things

00:17:21.710 --> 00:17:24.349
that matter, which is your
actual application development,

00:17:24.349 --> 00:17:27.680
and let Google do the heavy
lifting for Kubernetes.

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:28.180
Cool.

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:29.140
With that, I'm going
to hand it back

00:17:29.140 --> 00:17:30.820
to Anthony, who's
going to go into much

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:33.020
more depth on the build
and deploy lifecycle.

00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:34.240
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Awesome.

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:35.320
Thanks Wes.

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:37.420
So 18 minutes into
the session, and we

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:40.431
finally have reached
the title of our talk.

00:17:40.431 --> 00:17:42.930
So we're going to talk about
getting started with containers

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:44.740
in Kubernetes Engine.

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:47.950
And these are the key concepts
that we'll be discussing today,

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:50.020
all focused, again, around
the second narrative

00:17:50.020 --> 00:17:52.750
of actually using and
deploying things to Kubernetes.

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:59.020
So let's start with building
and deploying containers.

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:00.790
So when it comes to
this space, there

00:18:00.790 --> 00:18:03.320
are two key areas
we want to consider.

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:06.520
One is enabling
developers and two

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:09.400
is automating deployment
pipelines into environments

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:11.320
like staging or production.

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:13.460
And both are important.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:15.410
But let's start
with the developers.

00:18:15.410 --> 00:18:17.890
So for developers who
are new to technologies,

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:20.140
like containers and
Kubernetes, this

00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:23.030
is often what the workflow
feels like initially.

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:24.280
There's a lot of unknowns.

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:29.650
The magic in the middle could
be Docker Run, kubectl apply,

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:34.200
bash scripts, makefiles, a whole
bunch of different approaches.

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:37.020
And this can be a bad time
for developers, right?

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:40.710
What you end up with is a
bunch of differing workflows

00:18:40.710 --> 00:18:43.350
with long feedback loops
between code change

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:45.270
and actually being
able to interact

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:48.720
with their application running
in its runtime environment.

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:52.530
And so there's overhead of
maintaining this workflow.

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:54.510
There's no consistency
across what should

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:57.125
be a standard set of steps.

00:18:57.125 --> 00:18:59.250
This means that each
developer has their own method

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:00.280
of running locally.

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:02.640
And this makes reusing
and replicating things

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:05.470
in the development
lifecycle really hard.

00:19:05.470 --> 00:19:10.350
And so, if portability is one
of the key tenets of Kubernetes

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:12.360
and containers, then
this is a problem

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:14.970
that needs to be solved.

00:19:14.970 --> 00:19:17.220
And so that's where
skaffold comes into play.

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:20.240
So if you remember, skaffold
was the command that we--

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:23.810
skaffold was a tool that we used
to get our quiz application up

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:25.420
and running on GKE.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:29.150
Now, skaffold is perfect for
standardizing and enabling

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:32.240
iterative development with
containers in Kubernetes.

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:34.070
So with skaffold,
all developers have

00:19:34.070 --> 00:19:38.240
to do is focus on step one,
working with their code.

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:41.090
Skaffold takes care of the
rest of the steps in the middle

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:43.640
and will not only give that end
point back to the developer,

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:45.530
but will actually
stream out logs

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:49.130
to the developer to be able
to allow them to debug or make

00:19:49.130 --> 00:19:50.190
changes.

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:52.580
So, let's actually
switch back into the demo

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:57.140
and revisit our
quiz application.

00:19:57.140 --> 00:20:00.035
So here I am with--

00:20:00.035 --> 00:20:00.910
back in the terminal.

00:20:00.910 --> 00:20:03.280
And what we see here
is that skaffold dev--

00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:06.220
this is a command
that will actually

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:08.750
continue to run once our
application has been deployed.

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:10.750
And you'll see here that
it's actually streaming

00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:12.304
back logs for my application.

00:20:12.304 --> 00:20:14.470
Now, what's pretty cool is
that what it's also doing

00:20:14.470 --> 00:20:16.190
is watching for changes.

00:20:16.190 --> 00:20:18.550
So if I actually want to
change that quiz application,

00:20:18.550 --> 00:20:21.704
we're not having
to re kind of go

00:20:21.704 --> 00:20:23.620
through the process of
building our containers

00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:25.840
and then applying those
Kubernetes manifest.

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:28.010
All we have to do is
focus on the code.

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:31.680
So let's actually go
into the application.

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:32.620
Let's see here.

00:20:35.884 --> 00:20:38.300
So we'll see here we're back
at our application directory.

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:44.015
So I'm actually going to go
into the index HTML file.

00:20:44.015 --> 00:20:45.640
I'm going to make
some serious changes.

00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:47.560
And there's nothing
more nerve racking

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.616
than using VM in front
of 500 engineers.

00:20:50.616 --> 00:20:52.630
But what I'm going to do is--

00:20:52.630 --> 00:20:55.300
let's say that-- you will
see here I initially wanted

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:58.630
to quiz Wes, but maybe I want to
quiz everyone in the audience.

00:20:58.630 --> 00:21:03.860
So what we're going to do here
is change this and say, hello,

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:06.650
IO124 attendees.

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:09.350
Oh, spelling is hard.

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:11.710
All right, cool.

00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:16.200
So we're going to
change that file.

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:18.539
And great, I've changed my code.

00:21:18.539 --> 00:21:19.830
And notice what I haven't done.

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:21.580
I haven't gone into
any Docker file.

00:21:21.580 --> 00:21:23.490
I haven't gone
into Kube Control.

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:26.180
But what I have is this
skaffold dev process

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:28.700
that's continuously watching
for changes in my code.

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:30.980
And now, we've already
completed our deploy

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:34.100
and build process
within, oh, 12 seconds.

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:36.900
And so our application is
now up and running again.

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:40.630
And so if we revisit it, we
see that our code has actually

00:21:40.630 --> 00:21:42.490
changed behind
that same endpoint.

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:45.602
Is anyone brave enough
to take this one?

00:21:45.602 --> 00:21:47.060
It's the one on
the left, I'll do--

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:49.630
I'll-- oh, my goodness,
it's the one on the right.

00:21:49.630 --> 00:21:50.530
OK, well--

00:21:50.530 --> 00:21:52.360
[CROWD LAUGHS]

00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:53.560
--that was not planned.

00:21:57.050 --> 00:21:57.580
OK, great.

00:21:57.580 --> 00:21:59.440
So let's actually cut
back to the slides.

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:08.310
So skaffold-- let's see, sorry.

00:22:08.310 --> 00:22:10.850
So skaffold is actually
a pluggable architecture.

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:13.900
So one thing to note about that
is that while you saw me deploy

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:17.200
into a Kubernetes Engine cluster
that was running in the cloud,

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:19.810
we actually use skaffold
with Kubernetes environments

00:22:19.810 --> 00:22:21.450
that are running
locally in our laptop.

00:22:21.450 --> 00:22:23.260
So that's things like minikube.

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:26.680
This would result in a much
faster iterative deployment

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:27.250
process.

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:29.815
But it all depends on the
culture of your development

00:22:29.815 --> 00:22:31.690
teams, and if you're
doing remote development

00:22:31.690 --> 00:22:32.870
or local development.

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:37.270
What we can also do is not only
use our local Docker daemon.

00:22:37.270 --> 00:22:39.760
So to build these
Docker containers,

00:22:39.760 --> 00:22:42.520
I actually was using
Docker on my laptop.

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:44.830
But what you can do is
actually begin to outsource

00:22:44.830 --> 00:22:47.380
and push some of that heavy
lifting, save our battery,

00:22:47.380 --> 00:22:50.290
with tools like Google
Cloud Build, which

00:22:50.290 --> 00:22:51.910
is our manage build service.

00:22:51.910 --> 00:22:54.790
So skaffold is a
really useful tool

00:22:54.790 --> 00:23:01.320
that makes for very happy
and productive developers.

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:04.140
Skaffold can also work well
for our automated deployment

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:06.090
pipelines, but
we'll get into that.

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:09.600
First, we want to
dive into a few tips.

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:11.340
So throughout the
session, we'll just

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:12.780
be sprinkling in,
if you are just

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:14.696
getting started, what
are some things that you

00:23:14.696 --> 00:23:15.910
have to keep in mind?

00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:19.260
And so our first tips are around
actually building containers.

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:21.640
We have a tip on performance
and a tip on security.

00:23:21.640 --> 00:23:24.300
So first, around performance,
make sure that you slim

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:25.750
down your container images.

00:23:25.750 --> 00:23:28.860
So if you're coming from the
land of running everything

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:31.880
in virtual machines, it can be
tempting to stuff everything

00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:36.700
that we want into containers
and treat them like VMs.

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:39.060
That also makes for
a bad time because it

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:40.650
makes for slower pole times.

00:23:40.650 --> 00:23:43.920
And if containers--
if their value prop

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:46.770
is really around increasing
deployment velocity,

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:49.230
slow image pull times
will only hamper teams,

00:23:49.230 --> 00:23:52.020
and especially if you're
working at the scale of hundreds

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:55.290
if not thousands of
containers and microservices.

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:57.480
So an approach to slim
down container images

00:23:57.480 --> 00:23:59.730
is to actually use
multistage build.

00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:03.240
This is basically an approach
to build your binaries in one

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:06.270
container and then
copy that final binary

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:08.670
to another container
without all of the

00:24:08.670 --> 00:24:11.640
build tools, SDKs, or
other dependencies that

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:13.560
were needed for the build--

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:15.416
to build that artifact.

00:24:15.416 --> 00:24:17.040
Once you do this,
then you can actually

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:19.830
deploy things faster as we have
a more lightweight container

00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:20.980
image.

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:24.330
The second tip here is scan
your images for vulnerabilities.

00:24:24.330 --> 00:24:27.270
So Google's Hosted
Container Registries Service

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:29.280
provides vulnerability scanning.

00:24:29.280 --> 00:24:30.480
This is an alpha feature.

00:24:30.480 --> 00:24:33.090
But basically what this
does is that anything

00:24:33.090 --> 00:24:34.800
that you push to a Docker--

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:37.290
to the Container
Registry in Google

00:24:37.290 --> 00:24:39.950
will actually be scanned
with this feature enabled.

00:24:39.950 --> 00:24:42.810
And so you can also
enable it to send you

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:47.580
notifications should there be
a vulnerability via Pub/Sub.

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:50.500
Along those same lines
when getting started,

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:53.220
there are a swath of
public images out there--

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:55.637
an example is public
Docker Hub repos--

00:24:55.637 --> 00:24:57.720
that are awesome for
learning and awesome for just

00:24:57.720 --> 00:24:58.675
experimenting with.

00:24:58.675 --> 00:25:01.050
But they introduce inherent
risk if we start to take some

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:03.300
of these public containers--
they're untrusted

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:04.470
and unverified--

00:25:04.470 --> 00:25:07.500
and actually implement them
into our application stack.

00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:11.200
So avoid doing that.

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:14.365
So now that we have
containers, we're still--

00:25:14.365 --> 00:25:16.740
now that we have a container
workflow for our developers,

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:19.800
we're still missing how
we deploy containers

00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:22.290
into production at
scale in Kubernetes.

00:25:22.290 --> 00:25:24.810
So if the angle of
containers in Kubernetes

00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:27.720
is to get teams to move
faster, than a large part

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:30.330
of this actual magic starts
happening when we have

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:32.490
automated build and
deployment pipelines

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:36.550
across environments for
staging or production.

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:40.720
Now, let me preface
this with the opinion

00:25:40.720 --> 00:25:43.960
that there's no one right way
to do continuous integration

00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:47.937
and continuous delivery,
also known as CI/CD,

00:25:47.937 --> 00:25:50.020
but we can point to a few
patterns with an example

00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:51.470
pipeline here.

00:25:51.470 --> 00:25:53.740
So if a developer is
pushing to a feature branch

00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:56.530
or maybe tagging a release, we
should expect the developer's

00:25:56.530 --> 00:25:58.204
workflow to end there.

00:25:58.204 --> 00:26:00.870
It should end up in a Kubernetes
environment with a provided end

00:26:00.870 --> 00:26:03.130
point assuming tests pass.

00:26:03.130 --> 00:26:04.630
And the way that this--

00:26:04.630 --> 00:26:07.510
the way that it does this should
be an implementation detail

00:26:07.510 --> 00:26:10.480
and actually hidden away
from the developers.

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:13.150
So in this scenario, we're
using Cloud Build again.

00:26:13.150 --> 00:26:15.010
In this case, Cloud
Build has built

00:26:15.010 --> 00:26:19.180
triggers that can kick off
a build with multiple steps.

00:26:19.180 --> 00:26:22.270
Those steps can actually
include building the binaries,

00:26:22.270 --> 00:26:25.690
maybe running unit tests,
building the container image,

00:26:25.690 --> 00:26:28.819
and finally pushing
that out to a registry.

00:26:28.819 --> 00:26:31.360
Google Cloud Build is awesome
because it is able to provision

00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:35.410
and manage multiple steps either
sequentially or in parallel.

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:37.330
And so each of
these steps actually

00:26:37.330 --> 00:26:39.872
is executed within
a Docker container.

00:26:39.872 --> 00:26:41.330
And then across
all of these steps,

00:26:41.330 --> 00:26:42.805
we have a shared workspace.

00:26:42.805 --> 00:26:45.340
With that being
said, there are a lot

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:50.076
of other tools in the ecosystem
that can fulfill this CI need.

00:26:50.076 --> 00:26:52.450
And some of these may already
be present in your existing

00:26:52.450 --> 00:26:54.070
workflows.

00:26:54.070 --> 00:26:57.720
Jenkins is a popular one,
for example, that we see.

00:26:57.720 --> 00:27:00.180
So once we have this newly
tagged image in our registry,

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:02.263
we can now have that trigger
a deployment pipeline

00:27:02.263 --> 00:27:03.570
for a continuous delivery.

00:27:03.570 --> 00:27:06.210
So Cloud Build,
Jenkins, these tools

00:27:06.210 --> 00:27:08.070
can also perform
this functionality.

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:09.840
But in this example,
we're using a tool

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:12.199
called Spinnaker to perform
our continuous delivery.

00:27:12.199 --> 00:27:13.740
It's an open source
tool from Netflix

00:27:13.740 --> 00:27:16.830
that Google is
heavily invested in.

00:27:16.830 --> 00:27:19.200
Any CD tools should be able
to have a pipeline triggered

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:23.370
via a newly detected image
and kickoff additional steps

00:27:23.370 --> 00:27:25.770
to check out Kubernetes
manifest, maybe deploy

00:27:25.770 --> 00:27:27.900
them in two different
environments like staging,

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:28.980
production.

00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:31.170
Spinnaker here gives
us additional features

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:32.700
like execution windows.

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:35.820
Maybe we only want to automate
deployment at a specific time.

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:38.220
Or maybe we will want to
enforce manual approvals

00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:40.980
from our operations team before
releasing into production.

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:45.060
There's a whole bunch of
things that Spinnaker enables,

00:27:45.060 --> 00:27:48.180
safe rollbacks, being
able to have a nice UI

00:27:48.180 --> 00:27:49.770
around your actual pipeline.

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:51.570
And so it's important
to, again, when

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:53.550
looking at these
different tools,

00:27:53.550 --> 00:27:56.550
make sure that you're choosing
a tool not because of the hype

00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:59.230
but because it actually
fulfills the use cases that you

00:27:59.230 --> 00:28:00.302
are looking to achieve.

00:28:00.302 --> 00:28:02.010
Because like I said,
Cloud Build can also

00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:06.235
do this continuous delivery
and deployment phase.

00:28:06.235 --> 00:28:08.360
So a couple of tips when
deploying into production.

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:11.409
One, very simple, limit
access to production.

00:28:11.409 --> 00:28:12.200
You'd be surprised.

00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:13.741
I've seen a handful
of customers that

00:28:13.741 --> 00:28:18.500
will give Kube Control, kubectl
access to their developers

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:19.400
in production.

00:28:19.400 --> 00:28:22.760
And what we actually
want to do is

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:26.690
use some of GCP's native
isolation primitives.

00:28:26.690 --> 00:28:28.280
And so in this kind
of diagram, we're

00:28:28.280 --> 00:28:31.940
using a GCP project,
which owns IAM hierarchy,

00:28:31.940 --> 00:28:35.450
to actually limit access
to the Kubernetes cluster

00:28:35.450 --> 00:28:36.950
and running in production.

00:28:36.950 --> 00:28:39.590
So maybe we'll just give this
access to cluster operators

00:28:39.590 --> 00:28:42.050
and then give this access
to a service account

00:28:42.050 --> 00:28:43.850
for our CI/CD pipelines.

00:28:43.850 --> 00:28:46.850
And then second tip is to keep
your Kubernetes configuration

00:28:46.850 --> 00:28:47.940
as code.

00:28:47.940 --> 00:28:51.440
This is pretty key
because we'll want to,

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:53.330
one, store the existing state.

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:55.790
Maybe we are templatizing
our Kubernetes manifest

00:28:55.790 --> 00:28:58.455
and want to customize that
depending on each pipeline.

00:28:58.455 --> 00:29:00.830
But we also want to track a
history of applied manifests.

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:02.870
And Git and source
control management

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:06.100
is a great way to do that.

00:29:06.100 --> 00:29:08.170
So now that we've covered
building containers,

00:29:08.170 --> 00:29:09.265
we have to expose them.

00:29:09.265 --> 00:29:10.640
To be able to
interact with them,

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:13.780
how do we actually expose them
to internal or external users?

00:29:13.780 --> 00:29:15.940
So let's actually
jump back to the demo

00:29:15.940 --> 00:29:18.340
to take a look at how
Kubernetes networking works.

00:29:24.030 --> 00:29:27.180
Actually I'm going
to close this.

00:29:27.180 --> 00:29:29.110
So I'm just ending the
skaffold dev process

00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:32.200
that was running earlier.

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:34.480
And I actually want
that to be redeployed.

00:29:34.480 --> 00:29:36.701
So this will
actually not keep it

00:29:36.701 --> 00:29:38.200
in that iterative
development state.

00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:40.600
We're going to run and
deploy that application

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:43.100
without the feedback loop.

00:29:43.100 --> 00:29:48.110
But in the meantime, let's
actually switch our clusters,

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:49.160
so Kubernetes config.

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:57.665
So, we're actually get to
use the not-this cluster,

00:29:57.665 --> 00:29:58.970
Kubernetes cluster.

00:30:06.180 --> 00:30:06.930
OK.

00:30:06.930 --> 00:30:10.590
So, Kubernetes is
networking regardless of GKE

00:30:10.590 --> 00:30:13.200
or elsewhere requires
that a single--

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:15.494
every single pod gets
its own IP address.

00:30:15.494 --> 00:30:16.410
So let's look at that.

00:30:21.350 --> 00:30:22.190
It's kind of nice--

00:30:22.190 --> 00:30:24.020
if you look at other
orchestration tools,

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:27.320
these might actually require you
to do port mapping or something

00:30:27.320 --> 00:30:30.270
complex which at scale
can be really difficult.

00:30:30.270 --> 00:30:31.520
But you'll see here, we have--

00:30:31.520 --> 00:30:33.110
I'm actually
running-- I'm running

00:30:33.110 --> 00:30:35.810
the same application in a
different Kubernetes cluster.

00:30:35.810 --> 00:30:39.350
And I have three UI
pods and a database pod,

00:30:39.350 --> 00:30:41.570
and they all have
their own IP address.

00:30:41.570 --> 00:30:43.895
So to achieve this,
Kubernetes actually--

00:30:43.895 --> 00:30:46.977
the open source Kubernetes
doesn't enforce any opinions.

00:30:46.977 --> 00:30:48.560
And so there are
ways to achieve this.

00:30:48.560 --> 00:30:52.160
One is to hard code
routes per node.

00:30:52.160 --> 00:30:54.080
Another is to use
a network overlay

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:55.670
in place of routing tables.

00:30:55.670 --> 00:30:59.720
But ultimately, this can run
into limitations or overhead

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:03.120
to be able to just implement
basic networking features.

00:31:03.120 --> 00:31:06.320
But in GKE, we actually
get a VPC native way

00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:08.750
of allocating IP
addresses to pods.

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:11.570
So we can, within
our Google VPC,

00:31:11.570 --> 00:31:15.780
create a known range for
these pod IP addresses.

00:31:15.780 --> 00:31:17.960
These are known as IP aliases.

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:21.140
And then a slash
24 from that range

00:31:21.140 --> 00:31:23.440
will be assigned to each
node to give to pods

00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:24.690
that are running on that node.

00:31:24.690 --> 00:31:27.160
So we can actually look at that.

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:31.000
So I also have three
nodes in this cluster.

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:34.890
And we'll look at the pod CIDR
given to each of these nodes.

00:31:34.890 --> 00:31:37.530
And so you'll see here
that, again, what's

00:31:37.530 --> 00:31:40.260
important to note about this
is that these are natively

00:31:40.260 --> 00:31:44.010
implemented in GCP's SDN.

00:31:44.010 --> 00:31:45.900
We are not having to say--

00:31:45.900 --> 00:31:48.690
creating a route per node
to actually implement

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:51.570
this and say, if you're looking
for an address in this range

00:31:51.570 --> 00:31:52.850
here, go to this node.

00:31:52.850 --> 00:31:55.440
Instead, our VPC actually
handles that natively

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:56.580
and is aware of them.

00:31:56.580 --> 00:31:58.121
And so what's really
great about that

00:31:58.121 --> 00:32:01.890
is that this is useful when
we're bumping up against quotas

00:32:01.890 --> 00:32:03.964
for routes in large
clusters or for clusters

00:32:03.964 --> 00:32:05.880
with hybrid connectivity
where we can actually

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:08.790
advertise these ranges.

00:32:08.790 --> 00:32:11.610
But one thing that's core to
know about Kubernetes pods is

00:32:11.610 --> 00:32:13.350
that they can come
and go, which means

00:32:13.350 --> 00:32:15.060
that these IP addresses
that you see here

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:16.760
will actually also do the same.

00:32:16.760 --> 00:32:18.540
So Kubernetes gives
us a stable way

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:22.194
of fronting these pods with
a concept called services.

00:32:22.194 --> 00:32:24.360
So let's actually look at
some of our services here.

00:32:31.430 --> 00:32:35.230
So let's probably start
with our UI service.

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:36.790
Service types build
upon each other.

00:32:36.790 --> 00:32:40.300
You'll see here that we have
a base cluster IP service.

00:32:40.300 --> 00:32:43.420
And what this means is that
we have a stable virtual IP

00:32:43.420 --> 00:32:45.430
internal to our
Kubernetes cluster

00:32:45.430 --> 00:32:49.680
that fronts pods with this key
value-- these key value pairs.

00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:51.430
So we can actually
provide these key value

00:32:51.430 --> 00:32:53.170
pairs, otherwise
known as labels,

00:32:53.170 --> 00:32:55.150
in our Kubernetes manifest.

00:32:55.150 --> 00:32:58.150
And so this service is actually
associated with a dynamic map

00:32:58.150 --> 00:32:59.830
called Endpoints.

00:32:59.830 --> 00:33:03.160
So if we get Endpoints,
we'll actually

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:07.060
see here that I have a
map of all of the pod IPs

00:33:07.060 --> 00:33:08.290
that are running my UI.

00:33:08.290 --> 00:33:10.390
And this Endpoint
map is actually

00:33:10.390 --> 00:33:12.920
associated with the UI service.

00:33:12.920 --> 00:33:20.940
So if we scale that UI
deployment up and out pod,

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:22.000
how many replicas?

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:23.633
Anyone out there?

00:33:23.633 --> 00:33:24.133
Six?

00:33:24.133 --> 00:33:24.496
WESTON HUTCHINS: Six.

00:33:24.496 --> 00:33:25.870
ANTHONY BUSHONG: I
think we can that.

00:33:25.870 --> 00:33:26.710
Actually, I didn't--

00:33:26.710 --> 00:33:29.168
I don't think I enabled cluster
auto scale in this cluster,

00:33:29.168 --> 00:33:32.260
so let's keep it at four
and not go too wild.

00:33:32.260 --> 00:33:36.207
So if we actually look
at Endpoints again,

00:33:36.207 --> 00:33:38.040
we'll actually see some
of the control loops

00:33:38.040 --> 00:33:39.670
that Wes alluded to in action.

00:33:39.670 --> 00:33:42.570
And so we see that our
Endpoints object has dynamically

00:33:42.570 --> 00:33:46.290
been updated to incorporate
this new pod that was created.

00:33:46.290 --> 00:33:48.540
And so as we add more
and more pod replicas,

00:33:48.540 --> 00:33:50.314
we can expect this
map to be updated.

00:33:50.314 --> 00:33:52.230
And there's actually
some magic under the hood

00:33:52.230 --> 00:33:54.960
where we have a daemon
running on every single node,

00:33:54.960 --> 00:33:58.740
configuring IP tables on
all of our Kubernetes nodes

00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:03.810
to forward these
stable VPs to this--

00:34:03.810 --> 00:34:06.850
one of these pod IPs
in the Endpoints map.

00:34:06.850 --> 00:34:07.990
And so this is really good.

00:34:07.990 --> 00:34:10.531
We don't have to think about
how we configure load balancing.

00:34:10.531 --> 00:34:15.360
Kubernetes makes this so just
by declaring our service object.

00:34:15.360 --> 00:34:17.610
What I also want to call
out is that, like I said,

00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:18.969
services build upon each other.

00:34:18.969 --> 00:34:22.170
So we have an external IP
address for our UI pods

00:34:22.170 --> 00:34:23.080
as well.

00:34:23.080 --> 00:34:27.120
And so if we actually
go into the UI,

00:34:27.120 --> 00:34:29.130
we'll see that we'll
have network load

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:31.510
balancers provisioned for us.

00:34:31.510 --> 00:34:33.810
And then these will actually
route to all of the nodes

00:34:33.810 --> 00:34:36.090
in our cluster-- so each
cluster has three nodes--

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:38.639
and then we're back to
the IP tables forwarding

00:34:38.639 --> 00:34:41.310
to our actual pod traffic.

00:34:41.310 --> 00:34:43.920
So we've actually talked
a lot about how we

00:34:43.920 --> 00:34:46.080
expose an individual service.

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:48.355
But what happens when we
have multiple services, which

00:34:48.355 --> 00:34:49.980
I'm sure you're bound
to have if you're

00:34:49.980 --> 00:34:51.357
running Kubernetes engine?

00:34:51.357 --> 00:34:53.190
How do we have a single
entry point to route

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:53.961
to those services?

00:34:53.961 --> 00:34:55.710
So can we actually cut
back to the slides?

00:35:02.840 --> 00:35:05.810
So that's where the Kubernetes
Ingress object comes into play.

00:35:05.810 --> 00:35:08.230
So in GKE, the
default Ingress object

00:35:08.230 --> 00:35:11.706
is actually implemented as
our global HTTPS low balancer.

00:35:11.706 --> 00:35:13.330
And so if you create
an Ingress object,

00:35:13.330 --> 00:35:16.870
you'll see here that I have a
Service Foo and a Service Bar,

00:35:16.870 --> 00:35:18.790
we're actually able
to route users.

00:35:18.790 --> 00:35:21.110
One method is route by path.

00:35:21.110 --> 00:35:23.110
So if we're going
to path Foo, we're

00:35:23.110 --> 00:35:24.970
routed to our Service Foo.

00:35:24.970 --> 00:35:27.400
And then if we
route to path Bar,

00:35:27.400 --> 00:35:29.670
we're routed to our Service Bar.

00:35:29.670 --> 00:35:34.040
And so we also expect this
to do a lot of basic things

00:35:34.040 --> 00:35:36.410
at layer 7, like
TLS termination.

00:35:36.410 --> 00:35:38.540
But what's really cool
about GKE is that now we

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:41.390
get to, again, like Wes
mentioned, integrate

00:35:41.390 --> 00:35:45.050
some of the great GCP
services with our Kubernetes

00:35:45.050 --> 00:35:46.170
applications.

00:35:46.170 --> 00:35:49.010
So if we're using the
global HTTPS load balancer,

00:35:49.010 --> 00:35:52.190
we're actually able to use
things like Cloud Armor

00:35:52.190 --> 00:35:54.560
to protect against DOS attacks.

00:35:54.560 --> 00:35:57.620
Or we're able to use Identity
Wear Proxy, which is basically

00:35:57.620 --> 00:36:01.040
enforcing identity-based
access control in front

00:36:01.040 --> 00:36:02.687
of services, Cloud CDN.

00:36:02.687 --> 00:36:04.520
And then I want to call
out one thing that's

00:36:04.520 --> 00:36:07.280
really cool and very unique
to Google Cloud Platform,

00:36:07.280 --> 00:36:08.660
and that's Kubemci.

00:36:08.660 --> 00:36:10.661
So what we can actually
do, I think as you--

00:36:10.661 --> 00:36:12.410
this might not be a
getting started topic,

00:36:12.410 --> 00:36:15.140
but certainly a lot of customers
have directed there or headed

00:36:15.140 --> 00:36:18.140
in that direction-- is
running multiple clusters

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:19.641
in different geo regions.

00:36:19.641 --> 00:36:21.140
And so with Kubemci,
we can actually

00:36:21.140 --> 00:36:23.360
configure our load
balancer not only

00:36:23.360 --> 00:36:25.760
to front multiple
services, but to actually

00:36:25.760 --> 00:36:29.570
front multiple different
clusters across the globe.

00:36:29.570 --> 00:36:32.610
And so just a
quick demo of that.

00:36:32.610 --> 00:36:33.987
You'll see here that I have a--

00:36:33.987 --> 00:36:35.070
can we switch to the demo?

00:36:37.692 --> 00:36:40.150
So you'll see here that I have
two Kubernetes clusters, one

00:36:40.150 --> 00:36:43.730
running in US-West and run
one running in Europe-West.

00:36:43.730 --> 00:36:52.200
And so we also have our
global load balancer

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:54.070
the has a single IP address.

00:36:54.070 --> 00:36:56.310
So we have a single
entry point that

00:36:56.310 --> 00:36:59.250
is fronting both of our
Kubernetes Engine clusters.

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:03.360
So if I go to this, because we
are in wonderful San Francisco,

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:07.410
we should actually be routed
to the US-West1-A cluster.

00:37:07.410 --> 00:37:11.100
And what's really cool
is that if we actually go

00:37:11.100 --> 00:37:16.200
into a Europe-West1-B virtual
machine and actually hit that

00:37:16.200 --> 00:37:19.410
same endpoint, we're going
to be-- we should, assuming,

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:20.730
I don't want to jinx the demo--

00:37:20.730 --> 00:37:22.800
we should be routed to
our Europe-West cluster.

00:37:22.800 --> 00:37:25.050
And what's really great about
this is that previously,

00:37:25.050 --> 00:37:26.549
a lot of people
were trying to think

00:37:26.549 --> 00:37:29.100
about how to implement
this at the DNS layer.

00:37:29.100 --> 00:37:32.130
But with Kubernetes Engine
and our global load balancer,

00:37:32.130 --> 00:37:36.150
we can actually think about
this again at layer 7.

00:37:36.150 --> 00:37:43.940
So if we ping this,
we'll actually

00:37:43.940 --> 00:37:46.580
see that we hit that
same IP address.

00:37:46.580 --> 00:37:49.100
And we were served from
Europe-West1-B. So no

00:37:49.100 --> 00:37:50.040
tricks up my sleeves.

00:37:50.040 --> 00:37:52.280
This is something that
is just really cool

00:37:52.280 --> 00:37:56.870
and, again, built into our
actual global load balancer

00:37:56.870 --> 00:37:59.980
using any cast protocol.

00:37:59.980 --> 00:38:00.480
Great.

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:04.670
So going back to the
slides, the last thing

00:38:04.670 --> 00:38:08.060
I want to wrap up with
as far as load balancing

00:38:08.060 --> 00:38:12.530
is really making containers
a first class citizen.

00:38:12.530 --> 00:38:14.690
So as I mentioned
before with IP aliases,

00:38:14.690 --> 00:38:18.320
we're able to front IP addresses
with known IP addresses

00:38:18.320 --> 00:38:19.314
to the VPC.

00:38:19.314 --> 00:38:21.980
And what we can actually do with
that now is implement something

00:38:21.980 --> 00:38:23.584
called network Endpoint groups.

00:38:23.584 --> 00:38:25.000
So as I mentioned
before, there is

00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:28.710
a lot of IP tables magic in
traditional load balancing

00:38:28.710 --> 00:38:31.760
in Kubernetes, which could lead
to a bunch of extra network

00:38:31.760 --> 00:38:34.100
hops and really a
suboptimal path.

00:38:34.100 --> 00:38:36.000
And I think if you
look on the right here,

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:39.500
we'll see because we are
aware of pod IP addresses,

00:38:39.500 --> 00:38:43.640
we're actually able to configure
them as back ends to our load

00:38:43.640 --> 00:38:46.460
balancers in Google
Cloud Platform.

00:38:46.460 --> 00:38:50.000
And so what this makes for
is for an experience that

00:38:50.000 --> 00:38:53.180
makes load balancing much like
the way we do it across VMs.

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:55.930
We're able to health check
against the individual pods.

00:38:55.930 --> 00:38:58.550
We're able to route directly
to them without being rerouted

00:38:58.550 --> 00:38:59.990
through IP tables rules.

00:38:59.990 --> 00:39:03.800
And it really, again,
demonstrates unique properties

00:39:03.800 --> 00:39:05.680
of Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:39:05.680 --> 00:39:08.180
This is a feature in Alpha, but
certainly keep an eye on it.

00:39:10.710 --> 00:39:12.690
So the last tip I
have around networking

00:39:12.690 --> 00:39:16.470
is sometimes we need private
and internal applications

00:39:16.470 --> 00:39:17.730
to run on Kubernetes.

00:39:17.730 --> 00:39:19.830
And so Google
Kubernetes Engine also

00:39:19.830 --> 00:39:22.340
helps you to get enabled with
that via private clusters

00:39:22.340 --> 00:39:23.820
and internal load balancers.

00:39:23.820 --> 00:39:26.430
So private clusters means that
all of our virtual machines

00:39:26.430 --> 00:39:29.280
running our containers
are actually

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:32.100
running in a private
VPC without any access

00:39:32.100 --> 00:39:35.160
to the public internet, so we
can really harden our clusters

00:39:35.160 --> 00:39:37.140
using private clusters.

00:39:37.140 --> 00:39:39.450
And if we have
other applications

00:39:39.450 --> 00:39:42.000
in the same VPC that are not
running in Kubernetes Engine,

00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:44.310
we can actually also have
network load balancers

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:45.650
that are internal to a VPC.

00:39:49.870 --> 00:39:51.746
So moving on to
the next session.

00:39:51.746 --> 00:39:53.370
Now that we have our
app in containers,

00:39:53.370 --> 00:39:55.740
and we have a way for
people to access them,

00:39:55.740 --> 00:39:58.957
how do we handle a scenario
when a lot more people

00:39:58.957 --> 00:39:59.790
want to access them?

00:39:59.790 --> 00:40:02.890
And that's where
autoscaling comes into play.

00:40:02.890 --> 00:40:04.390
So before we talk
about autoscaling,

00:40:04.390 --> 00:40:07.390
it's paramount to introduce a
concept of requests and limits.

00:40:07.390 --> 00:40:10.990
The first tip here is use them.

00:40:10.990 --> 00:40:13.360
They define a floor and
ceiling for containers

00:40:13.360 --> 00:40:16.664
in pods around resources
like CPU and memory.

00:40:16.664 --> 00:40:18.580
And they actually inform
how our control plane

00:40:18.580 --> 00:40:20.560
schedules pods across nodes.

00:40:20.560 --> 00:40:23.290
And you'll see that they
actually inform autoscaling.

00:40:23.290 --> 00:40:26.680
A quick note here is that if
you define limits for CPU,

00:40:26.680 --> 00:40:28.870
your containers will be
throttled at that limit.

00:40:28.870 --> 00:40:30.370
But for memory,
it's incompressible,

00:40:30.370 --> 00:40:32.453
and so they will actually
be killed and restarted.

00:40:32.453 --> 00:40:34.780
So really, again,
it does require

00:40:34.780 --> 00:40:38.470
a lot of understanding of
what are acceptable resource

00:40:38.470 --> 00:40:40.858
utilization of your containers.

00:40:43.520 --> 00:40:46.210
So the two most well-known
autoscaling paradigms

00:40:46.210 --> 00:40:49.060
are horizontal pod autoscaling
and cluster autoscaling.

00:40:49.060 --> 00:40:50.590
So for horizontal
pod autoscaling,

00:40:50.590 --> 00:40:53.020
we can actually target
groups of pods and scale

00:40:53.020 --> 00:40:55.600
on metrics like CPU.

00:40:55.600 --> 00:40:59.020
Or in GKE, we can actually
provide horizontal pod

00:40:59.020 --> 00:41:00.970
autoscaling based
on custom metrics.

00:41:00.970 --> 00:41:02.350
So if you're doing
something like a task queue,

00:41:02.350 --> 00:41:04.390
and you want to track
that and scale more pods

00:41:04.390 --> 00:41:06.760
to handle, let's
say, more work, you

00:41:06.760 --> 00:41:10.550
can configure HPA to do that.

00:41:10.550 --> 00:41:12.910
We also have the concept
of cluster autoscaling.

00:41:12.910 --> 00:41:16.570
So eventually if you're scaling
a numerous amount of pods,

00:41:16.570 --> 00:41:18.910
you'll eventually need to
add more physical-- or not

00:41:18.910 --> 00:41:21.910
physical-- virtual resources
to your Kubernetes cluster

00:41:21.910 --> 00:41:23.270
to run those pods.

00:41:23.270 --> 00:41:25.800
And that's where cluster
autoscaling comes into play.

00:41:25.800 --> 00:41:28.420
Pending pods that
need resources--

00:41:28.420 --> 00:41:30.900
maybe they're not
able to be scheduled--

00:41:30.900 --> 00:41:33.010
will trigger cluster
autoscaling to spin out

00:41:33.010 --> 00:41:36.900
more machines in our new pools.

00:41:36.900 --> 00:41:40.760
And so these two autoscaling
paradigms are relatively new,

00:41:40.760 --> 00:41:42.510
but I do think it's
important to call out.

00:41:42.510 --> 00:41:45.840
One is being able to scale
workloads vertically.

00:41:45.840 --> 00:41:48.480
So if we want-- if we think of
a compute intensive application,

00:41:48.480 --> 00:41:53.970
maybe like a database, adding
more horizontal replicas

00:41:53.970 --> 00:41:55.770
won't necessarily
solve our issue.

00:41:55.770 --> 00:41:57.690
And so maybe we actually
need to scale up

00:41:57.690 --> 00:42:00.906
the amount of resources that
that individual pod has.

00:42:00.906 --> 00:42:02.280
So with vertical
pod autoscaling,

00:42:02.280 --> 00:42:03.738
you'll actually
get recommendations

00:42:03.738 --> 00:42:05.880
that can be either
suggested or automatically

00:42:05.880 --> 00:42:09.330
applied to resize the
requests of your pods.

00:42:09.330 --> 00:42:11.880
And then on the right-- or
on the-- yeah, on the right

00:42:11.880 --> 00:42:14.760
here, you'll see that
we also have the ability

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:16.770
to scale infrastructure
dynamically.

00:42:16.770 --> 00:42:19.207
And so what this means
is that if we're--

00:42:19.207 --> 00:42:21.540
if we have a certain set of
node pools in our Kubernetes

00:42:21.540 --> 00:42:23.529
cluster, but we are--

00:42:23.529 --> 00:42:25.320
we don't have any
resources to accommodate,

00:42:25.320 --> 00:42:28.050
let's say, a 16-CPU
request, we could actually

00:42:28.050 --> 00:42:30.240
use something like
node auto provisioning

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:35.250
to deploy a new node pool to
handle that request that you

00:42:35.250 --> 00:42:37.480
can scale out as many
one-CPU machines as you want,

00:42:37.480 --> 00:42:38.830
it's not going to fix that.

00:42:38.830 --> 00:42:40.440
So node auto
provisioning really helps

00:42:40.440 --> 00:42:42.960
customers that are
operating at scale

00:42:42.960 --> 00:42:45.750
and can't necessarily keep
track of every single one

00:42:45.750 --> 00:42:48.030
of their hundreds of
microservices and requests

00:42:48.030 --> 00:42:52.860
and to have node pools that
fit each of those workloads.

00:42:52.860 --> 00:42:53.494
Awesome.

00:42:53.494 --> 00:42:54.910
So high availability
is important.

00:42:54.910 --> 00:42:56.285
And I'm now going
to kick it over

00:42:56.285 --> 00:42:57.720
to Wes to tell us more about it.

00:42:57.720 --> 00:42:59.221
WESTON HUTCHINS:
Awesome So I'll try

00:42:59.221 --> 00:43:01.344
to go through this relatively
quickly because we're

00:43:01.344 --> 00:43:02.560
running a little low on time.

00:43:02.560 --> 00:43:05.527
So HA, we added a
number of new features,

00:43:05.527 --> 00:43:07.110
actually in the last
couple of months,

00:43:07.110 --> 00:43:09.250
around high availability
applications.

00:43:09.250 --> 00:43:12.570
The first one is we added
regional persistent disk

00:43:12.570 --> 00:43:14.550
support to GKE.

00:43:14.550 --> 00:43:16.590
Quite simply what
this does is it

00:43:16.590 --> 00:43:20.640
replicates your persistent disks
across two zones in a region.

00:43:20.640 --> 00:43:21.900
Now, why is this nice?

00:43:21.900 --> 00:43:23.940
Well, you don't have
to handle replication

00:43:23.940 --> 00:43:26.550
at the application layer
and worry about distributed

00:43:26.550 --> 00:43:27.090
databases.

00:43:27.090 --> 00:43:29.370
You can just store
it all to a PD,

00:43:29.370 --> 00:43:32.270
and Google Cloud Platform will
automatically replicate those

00:43:32.270 --> 00:43:33.370
to different zones.

00:43:33.370 --> 00:43:35.790
So the abstraction happens
at the storage layer,

00:43:35.790 --> 00:43:39.450
which you get out of
the box automatically.

00:43:39.450 --> 00:43:41.820
The other concept
around high availability

00:43:41.820 --> 00:43:44.050
is what we call
regional clusters.

00:43:44.050 --> 00:43:47.070
This is a multi-node
control plane

00:43:47.070 --> 00:43:51.210
that spreads both the
Kubernetes API server and all

00:43:51.210 --> 00:43:53.940
of the control plane
components along with etcd

00:43:53.940 --> 00:43:56.190
across three zones in a region.

00:43:56.190 --> 00:43:59.310
Now, the really nice thing
about regional clusters

00:43:59.310 --> 00:44:02.460
is that it increases the
uptime of the cluster

00:44:02.460 --> 00:44:07.630
from 2.5 nines to 3.5 nines and
you get zero downtime upgrades.

00:44:07.630 --> 00:44:09.330
So in the zonal
cluster case where

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:12.360
you have a single node
running your control plane,

00:44:12.360 --> 00:44:15.000
it will go down temporarily
when we upgrade the Kubernetes

00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:15.600
version.

00:44:15.600 --> 00:44:18.270
But if you are using regional
clusters for your production

00:44:18.270 --> 00:44:20.580
workloads, we'll do
a one-by-one upgrade.

00:44:20.580 --> 00:44:22.620
And we'll always
have two masters

00:44:22.620 --> 00:44:25.440
ready to serve any workloads
that come into the cluster.

00:44:25.440 --> 00:44:27.300
The best part about
this is it's available

00:44:27.300 --> 00:44:30.150
at no additional charge
over the zonal clusters.

00:44:30.150 --> 00:44:33.180
We really want everyone
to be able to use

00:44:33.180 --> 00:44:35.850
multi-master, high-visibility
clusters for their production

00:44:35.850 --> 00:44:37.950
apps.

00:44:37.950 --> 00:44:41.400
So a few quick tips on
upgrades just so people

00:44:41.400 --> 00:44:44.520
understand how
this works on GKE.

00:44:44.520 --> 00:44:47.310
For the control plane,
Google engineers

00:44:47.310 --> 00:44:49.420
will constantly keep
this up to date.

00:44:49.420 --> 00:44:51.420
We will upgrade behind
the scenes automatically.

00:44:51.420 --> 00:44:53.730
This is not something you
can control outside of a few

00:44:53.730 --> 00:44:55.938
maintenance Windows settings
that tell us when we can

00:44:55.938 --> 00:44:57.930
or cannot upgrade your cluster.

00:44:57.930 --> 00:45:00.750
Now, we almost never upgrade
to the latest version

00:45:00.750 --> 00:45:01.410
of Kubernetes.

00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:03.480
We'll upgrade you to a
more stable version that's

00:45:03.480 --> 00:45:06.240
had a bunch of patch fixes
and keep you on that,

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:07.770
and slowly roll
you up to the new--

00:45:07.770 --> 00:45:10.020
to the next version once
another version of Kubernetes

00:45:10.020 --> 00:45:11.310
comes out.

00:45:11.310 --> 00:45:13.080
You can also trigger
this manually

00:45:13.080 --> 00:45:15.270
if you really want the
latest version of Kubernetes

00:45:15.270 --> 00:45:18.510
by going to our Cloud
console and clicking upgrade.

00:45:18.510 --> 00:45:22.050
Now, the upgrade for the control
plane happens automatically.

00:45:22.050 --> 00:45:23.910
For nodes, if you
enable auto-upgrade,

00:45:23.910 --> 00:45:25.180
it'll work for that as well.

00:45:25.180 --> 00:45:27.820
However, if you don't
enable auto-upgrade,

00:45:27.820 --> 00:45:30.630
this is much more control over
when your Kubernetes nodes get

00:45:30.630 --> 00:45:32.034
upgraded to the next version.

00:45:32.034 --> 00:45:33.450
And the thing I
wanted to call out

00:45:33.450 --> 00:45:36.420
is that your cluster can
work in this mixed mode

00:45:36.420 --> 00:45:39.390
state via Kubernetes
backwards compatibility.

00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:42.660
So we can have nodes running on
1.8 and a master that's running

00:45:42.660 --> 00:45:43.920
on 1.10.

00:45:43.920 --> 00:45:47.500
And we actually won't get to
the next version of Kubernetes

00:45:47.500 --> 00:45:49.440
until we go and
upgrade the nodes.

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:51.090
Now, when you're
upgrading your nodes,

00:45:51.090 --> 00:45:52.830
there's a few things
to think about.

00:45:52.830 --> 00:45:55.920
You can do the rolling upgrade
that just happens by default.

00:45:55.920 --> 00:45:58.350
And this is just by
clicking the node upgrade

00:45:58.350 --> 00:45:59.700
button in the window.

00:45:59.700 --> 00:46:02.460
Or you can do something called
migration with node pools.

00:46:02.460 --> 00:46:05.340
There's a blog post about this
on the Google Cloud Platform

00:46:05.340 --> 00:46:07.330
blog that goes into
much more detail.

00:46:07.330 --> 00:46:09.630
But the short of it
is, you can actually

00:46:09.630 --> 00:46:13.020
create another node pool on the
newer version of Kubernetes,

00:46:13.020 --> 00:46:14.940
split traffic over
to that node pool

00:46:14.940 --> 00:46:19.200
using kubectl drain
and cordon, and then

00:46:19.200 --> 00:46:22.590
you can test a little bit of
the traffic on the new version.

00:46:22.590 --> 00:46:24.414
If you see your
app is misbehaving,

00:46:24.414 --> 00:46:25.830
you still have the
other node pool

00:46:25.830 --> 00:46:27.788
that you can route back
to the previous version

00:46:27.788 --> 00:46:28.860
while you debug.

00:46:28.860 --> 00:46:31.260
Once the new version looks
good, you scale that one up,

00:46:31.260 --> 00:46:32.730
and you scale the
other node pool down.

00:46:32.730 --> 00:46:34.680
And we actually see a lot
of production users doing

00:46:34.680 --> 00:46:37.140
this who want to have a much
more testable way of rolling

00:46:37.140 --> 00:46:40.330
to a new version of Kubernetes.

00:46:40.330 --> 00:46:43.650
And the last section is
logging and monitoring.

00:46:43.650 --> 00:46:45.720
So I wanted to call
out that at QCon

00:46:45.720 --> 00:46:47.340
in Europe this
year in Copenhagen,

00:46:47.340 --> 00:46:49.950
we announced Stackdriver
for Kubernetes.

00:46:49.950 --> 00:46:53.070
What this allows you to do is
take open source Prometheus

00:46:53.070 --> 00:46:56.550
instrumentation and inject
that into Stackdriver's UI.

00:46:56.550 --> 00:46:58.260
And the really cool
thing about this

00:46:58.260 --> 00:47:00.180
is it's multi-cluster
aggregated,

00:47:00.180 --> 00:47:02.638
and you get to use all of the
greatest Stackdriver features

00:47:02.638 --> 00:47:04.740
like the pre-built
dashboards and the ability

00:47:04.740 --> 00:47:06.630
to do things like set
alerts so that you're

00:47:06.630 --> 00:47:10.000
notified when something
goes wrong in your cluster.

00:47:10.000 --> 00:47:14.160
The other feature that we have
on GKE is GKE audit logging.

00:47:14.160 --> 00:47:16.560
So if people are
changing your cluster,

00:47:16.560 --> 00:47:19.440
or you want to figure out who
was the last person to deploy,

00:47:19.440 --> 00:47:23.430
you can go into our logging
UI under Stackdriver

00:47:23.430 --> 00:47:27.420
and see both pod-level and
deployment-level information

00:47:27.420 --> 00:47:29.800
along with information
about the resources as well.

00:47:29.800 --> 00:47:31.780
So if somebody added a
note to your cluster,

00:47:31.780 --> 00:47:33.405
this is the screen
that you would go to

00:47:33.405 --> 00:47:35.120
in order to figure that out.

00:47:35.120 --> 00:47:38.630
Now, there are a ton
more topics that we just

00:47:38.630 --> 00:47:41.720
didn't have enough time to
cover in 50 minutes, things

00:47:41.720 --> 00:47:44.474
like security, identity,
policy management.

00:47:44.474 --> 00:47:45.890
There's a lot of
sessions that are

00:47:45.890 --> 00:47:48.230
going on that cover these
topics in much more depth.

00:47:48.230 --> 00:47:51.260
Anthony and I will be outside
to help answer any lingering

00:47:51.260 --> 00:47:52.850
questions you have.

00:47:52.850 --> 00:47:54.680
With that, try out GKE.

00:47:54.680 --> 00:47:57.170
And thank you very much.

00:47:57.170 --> 00:48:00.220
[MUSIC PLAYING]

